{-| `Std.Ai.Train` — fine-tune a LoRA or QLoRA adapter from Sky code.

    import Std.Ai.Train as Train
    import Std.Ai.Dataset as Dataset

    supportJob : Dataset.Version Dataset.Chat -> Train.Job
    supportJob version =
        Train.lora Train.devBase version
            |> Train.withName "support"
            |> Train.withQuantisedBase
            |> Train.withRank 16
            |> Train.withSeed 7
            |> Train.withGgufExport

    -- A command-line program runs it in the foreground:
    Train.drive db (supportJob version)

A job trains on a frozen `Dataset.Version`, never on a live dataset, so its
lineage is fixed by the type: the adapter records the version's name and
digest, the base, the backend, the toolchain and the seed.

**The backend is not in the code by default.** On Apple silicon the detected
backend is `Mlx` (MLX-LM), on Linux with an NVIDIA GPU it is `Unsloth`, and
`SKY_AI_TRAIN_BACKEND` (`mlx`, `unsloth` or `fake`) overrides both and
`withBackend`. `Fake` is a scripted trainer for tests and CI: it runs the
whole protocol and writes a real, no-op adapter. A backend this host cannot
run is `NoAccelerator`, never a silent fallback.

**The Mlx trainer is native.** `Mlx` trains with `sky-trainer`, Sky's own
LoRA trainer (C on MLX), with no Python: a pinned, hash-checked bundle Sky
installs under the AI home, or the operator's own build named by
`SKY_AI_TRAINER_BIN`. `Unsloth` and `Fake` still run a Sky-shipped driver in
a managed Python toolchain (a pinned uv, a pinned CPython and a hash-locked
set of wheels). `provision` (and `sky ai provision --train mlx`) installs the
trainer ahead of time; a missing one with fetching off is `NotProvisioned`,
naming the command.

**Training is durable.** `workflow` is a `Std.Durable` workflow: resolve,
check, stage the data, provision, launch the trainer, watch it, convert the
adapter to GGUF, register it. The trainer runs detached in its own session,
so it outlives the program that launched it; a resumed workflow reattaches to
it. A trainer that dies is relaunched from its newest verified checkpoint, up
to `withMaxRelaunches` times. Checkpoints are safetensors with a manifest of
digests, never a pickle.

Failures are typed: `TrainingFailed`, `TrainingCancelled`, `ModelTooLarge`,
`NoAccelerator`, `DatasetInvalid`, `NotProvisioned`, `ToolchainFailed`,
`ChecksumMismatch` and `AdapterMismatch` (an adapter that does not fit the
serving model). See `docs/ai/train.md`.
-}

